INT. FUNERAL HOME - FAMILY ROOM - AFTERNOON

Dim lamps. A mahogany casket at the front. Folding chairs in careful rows. A box of tissues on every seat. The smell of lilies and carpet cleaner.

ROSEMARY PEMBERTON-DIALLO, 60s, in black wool, sits ramrod straight in the front row. Her son BENEDICT PEMBERTON, 40s, rumpled suit, fidgets beside her. Their cousin, DELPHINE ACHEBE-HART, 50s, large sunglasses indoors, stands against the back wall clutching her purse.

BENEDICT
Mother, I can handle it.

ROSEMARY
You can't handle a toaster.

BENEDICT
That was one time.

DELPHINE
(from the back)
It was three times, Ben.

BENEDICT
Stay out of it, Delphine.

DELPHINE
Don't tell me to stay out. I'm the one who found the will.

ROSEMARY
The will is none of your business.

DELPHINE
It's my business. I'm named in it.

BENEDICT
You are not.

DELPHINE
Page four. Paragraph two.

ROSEMARY
(rising)
That paragraph was struck.

DELPHINE
By who?

ROSEMARY
By me.

BENEDICT
Mother!

ROSEMARY
Be quiet, Benedict.

BENEDICT
You can't just --

ROSEMARY
I can do what I please. I'm his widow.

DELPHINE
And I'm his sister's daughter. Which makes me blood.

ROSEMARY
Blood is overrated.

DELPHINE
Says the woman who married into it.

Silence. Rosemary stares at the casket. Her shoulders sag. Benedict puts a hand on her arm. She lets it stay.

ROSEMARY
(quiet)
He would have hated this.

BENEDICT
He would have loved it. The shouting.

DELPHINE
(wiping her eyes behind the sunglasses)
He started half of it.

A beat. Rosemary lets out a short, surprised laugh. Benedict follows. Delphine, last, a sob that turns into a snort. They sit in the gentle ruin.

The door opens. FUNERAL DIRECTOR OSWALD PRICE, 50s, black suit, clears his throat.

OSWALD
Ready, whenever you are.

ROSEMARY
Give us a moment, Mr. Price.

OSWALD
Of course.

He retreats. Delphine crosses the room and sits on Rosemary's other side. Three of them in a row, looking at the casket.
